{
  "dom_id": "step-e13125",
  "name": "Example: Computing a Constant of Proportionality",
  "problem": "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $15$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $y=kx$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality?",
  "worked_solution": "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$",
